Setting: in this part of 'Analysis situs' (4th version, 1986) Grothendieck works with an abstract combinatorial structure M (an 'm-gérie'). M carries an order ≤ (X ≤ Y: X is a face of Y), auxiliary relations ≪ and ∘≪, and a relation X |∘| Y (meaning, roughly, that X and Y have disjoint interiors). He also has the concrete structure Fig(P) of 'figures' made of subsets of a set P. On the previous page he showed that a homomorphism f : M → Fig(P) is determined by the map X ↦ |X| := |f(X)| ∈ 𝔓(P), via f(X) = {|Y| : Y ∈ X̃}, where X̃ = {Y ≤ X}.

This page asks the converse question: which maps X ↦ |X| : M → 𝔓(P) come from such a morphism? He notes this brings him back to the situation of p.33.

Definitions. For Φ ⊂ M he sets |Φ| = ∪_{X∈Φ} |X|. Hence:
- the boundary: ∂X = X̃ − {X} = {Y < X}, and |∂X| = ∪_{Y<X} |Y| ⊂ |X|;
- the open cell: |X|° = |X| ∖ |∂X|.

Necessary conditions:
- (i) monotonicity: X ≪ Y ⇒ |X| ⊂ |Y|;
- (ii) X |∘| Y ⇒ |X|° ∩ |Y|° = ∅;
- (iii) every open cell is nonempty: |X|° ≠ ∅;
- (iv) each closed cell is the disjoint union of the open cells of its faces: |X| = ∪_{Y≤X} |Y|°.

He then sets out to show that these conditions already let one define f(X) ∈ Fig él(P), an elementary figure in the space |X|, by recipe (α) of the previous paragraph.

The next page completes this as follows:
- the family (|Y|)_{Y∈X̃} is an elementary figure, by condition (iii) of the proposition on p.37;
- the map X̃ → f(X) is an isomorphism compatible with ≤, so |X|° = f(X)°;
- (ii) gives preservation of |∘|.

What remains is preservation of ∘≤, i.e. X ∘≤ Y ⇒ |X|° ⊂ |Y|°.

In modern terms this is the axiomatisation of a 'geometric realisation' of an abstract face poset as a decomposition of a set into open cells indexed by the poset. The closure of each cell is the union of the cells below it, and cells are nonempty and pairwise disjoint where required. It is a set-theoretic analogue of a regular cell decomposition or a P-stratification with the frontier condition.
